.custom = .{ .doc_section = "CLI Reference" },
---
Pass Docker options before the image name and LLMD flags after it. The CUDA
example below shows how a host port and model directory reach LLMD.

# [Published port]($heading.id('port-path'))

```=html
<dl class="cli-docker-flow">
  <div><dt>Host address</dt><dd><code>127.0.0.1:8080</code></dd></div>
  <div><dt>Container port</dt><dd><code>8000/tcp</code></dd></div>
  <div><dt>LLMD listener</dt><dd><code>--listen=0.0.0.0:8000</code></dd></div>
</dl>
```

Connect from the host at `http://127.0.0.1:8080`. Docker forwards requests
to port `8000`, where LLMD listens inside the container.

# [Model directory]($heading.id('model-path'))

```=html
<dl class="cli-docker-flow">
  <div><dt>Host directory</dt><dd><code>${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8</code></dd></div>
  <div><dt>Container directory</dt><dd><code>/model</code> (read-only)</dd></div>
  <div><dt>LLMD model path</dt><dd><code>--model=/model</code></dd></div>
</dl>
```

# [Run a local model]($heading.id('run-a-local-checkpoint'))

Use **Download first** in the [CUDA quickstart preset]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart').unsafeRef('lfm3-fp8-cuda'))
to download the example model. If the files are elsewhere, change the mount's
`source` to your [local model directory]($link.page('llmd/cli-reference/model-and-listener-flags').ref('local-model-files')).

```=html
<div class="cli-docker-command-order" aria-label="Argument order">
  <span>Docker options</span><code>zmlai/llmd:cuda</code><span>LLMD flags</span>
</div>
```

```sh
docker run --rm --name llmd \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8000 \
  --gpus=all --shm-size=256GB \
  --mount "type=bind,source=${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8,target=/model,readonly" \
  zmlai/llmd:cuda \
  --model=/model \
  --listen=0.0.0.0:8000
```

# [General Docker options]($heading.id('docker-run-options'))

Images and daemon configurations can supply their own settings.
See the [Docker run reference](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/run/)
and [daemon defaults](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/dockerd/).

| Docker option | Default | Use with LLMD |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `-p`, `--publish` | No published host ports | `-p 127.0.0.1:8080:8000` publishes the container port on host loopback. Omitting the host address usually publishes to all host interfaces. |
| `--network`, `--net` | Default bridge on a standard Linux Docker host | The TPU recipe uses [host networking](https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/drivers/host/) and connects directly to the LLMD listener. |

## [Container lifecycle]($heading.id('container-lifecycle'))

| Docker option | Default | Use with LLMD |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--name` | Automatically generated | Use a fixed name for `docker logs` and `docker stop`. |
| `--rm` | Off | Remove the container when it exits. |
| `-d`, `--detach` | Off | Run in the background. Inspect startup with `docker logs <name>`. |
| `-i`, `-t` | Both off | Keep stdin open or allocate a terminal. Use them for interactive terminal sessions. |
| `--pull` | `missing` | Pull the image when it is absent locally. |
| `--restart` | `no` | Set a restart policy for a retained container. Docker rejects `--rm` with a restart policy. |

# [Platform recipe options]($heading.id('accelerators-and-shared-memory'))

Docker requests no extra accelerator devices by default. Use the device flags
for your backend. On Apple Silicon, use the native Homebrew build.

```=html
<div class="cli-platform-box" data-cli-options>
  <fieldset class="cli-platform-tabs command-tabs" aria-label="Docker platforms" data-cli-option-control hidden data-pagefind-ignore>
    <label class="command-tab"><input type="radio" name="docker-recipe-platform" value="cuda" aria-controls="docker-platform-cuda" checked><span><strong>CUDA</strong><small>NVIDIA GPU</small></span></label>
    <label class="command-tab"><input type="radio" name="docker-recipe-platform" value="rocm" aria-controls="docker-platform-rocm"><span><strong>ROCm</strong><small>AMD GPU</small></span></label>
    <label class="command-tab"><input type="radio" name="docker-recipe-platform" value="oneapi" aria-controls="docker-platform-oneapi"><span><strong>oneAPI</strong><small>Intel GPU</small></span></label>
    <label class="command-tab"><input type="radio" name="docker-recipe-platform" value="tpu" aria-controls="docker-platform-tpu"><span><strong>TPU</strong><small>Google TPU</small></span></label>
    <label class="command-tab"><input type="radio" name="docker-recipe-platform" value="furiosa" aria-controls="docker-platform-furiosa"><span><strong>Furiosa</strong><small>RNGD NPU</small></span></label>
  </fieldset>
  <section id="docker-platform-cuda" data-cli-option="cuda">
    <header><h3>CUDA</h3><code>zmlai/llmd:cuda</code></header>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--gpus=all</code> exposes NVIDIA GPUs through NVIDIA Container Toolkit. You can select specific GPUs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Optional shared memory</strong> <code>--shm-size=256GB</code></p>
  </section>
  <section id="docker-platform-rocm" data-cli-option="rocm">
    <header><h3>ROCm</h3><code>zmlai/llmd:rocm</code></header>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--device=/dev/kfd</code> exposes the AMD compute device.</li>
      <li><code>--device=/dev/dri</code> exposes the GPU device nodes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Optional shared memory</strong> <code>--shm-size=256GB</code></p>
  </section>
  <section id="docker-platform-oneapi" data-cli-option="oneapi">
    <header><h3>oneAPI</h3><code>zmlai/llmd:oneapi</code></header>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--device=/dev/dri</code> exposes the Intel GPU device nodes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="docker-platform-tpu" data-cli-option="tpu">
    <header><h3>TPU</h3><code>zmlai/llmd:tpu</code></header>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--net=host</code> shares the host network. Connect directly to the LLMD listener and omit port publishing.</li>
      <li><code>--privileged</code> grants broad host-device access in this recipe. Docker leaves it off by default.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="docker-platform-furiosa" data-cli-option="furiosa">
    <header><h3>Furiosa</h3><code>zmlai/llmd:furiosa</code></header>
    <p>The Docker recipe is pending hardware validation.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--device=furiosa.ai/npu=npu0</code> selects an NPU through the Container Device Interface (CDI).</li>
      <li><code>--device=/dev/dma_heap/system</code> exposes the system DMA heap.</li>
      <li><code>--security-opt=seccomp=unconfined</code> allows driver syscalls by disabling the container's seccomp filter. A custom profile that allows the required syscalls can retain filtering.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
```

`--shm-size` sets `/dev/shm`. Its default comes from the daemon, normally
`64 MiB`. The shared-memory value in a recipe is adjustable and can vary by model.

# [Optional model files and environment]($heading.id('environment-and-mounts'))

Pass these options before the image name when the container needs host files
or environment values. Docker adds no explicit mounts or environment overrides
unless requested.

| Docker option | Use with LLMD |
| --- | --- |
| `--mount …,target=/model,readonly` | Expose local weights at `/model`. Bind mounts are writable by default, so use `readonly` for weights. |
| `--mount …,target=/drafter,readonly` | Expose a local drafter at `/drafter` and pass that path to `--speculative-drafter-model`. |
| `-e HF_TOKEN` or `--env HF_TOKEN` | Forward the exported host value when Hugging Face access requires a token. |
| `-v HOST_DIR:/cache/furiosa` and `-e FURIOSA_COMPILER_CACHE=/cache/furiosa` | Persist the Furiosa compiler cache in a writable host directory when enabled. |

# [LLMD flags after the image]($heading.id('llmd-arguments'))

| LLMD flag | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `--model=/model` | Read the mounted directory inside the container. |
| `--listen=0.0.0.0:8000` | Bind HTTP inside the container. For bridge networking, match Docker's container port. |
| `--model-name=<text>` | Set the served model ID. With `--model=/model`, the default ID is `model`. |

See [Model and listener flags]($link.page('llmd/cli-reference/model-and-listener-flags'))
and [Environment variables]($link.page('llmd/cli-reference/environment-variables')) for other inputs.

Use the [LLMD quickstart]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart')) to generate a launch
command for your backend. See [Hardware compatibility]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/reference/hardware-compatibility'))
for host requirements and [Deploy LLMD with Docker]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/operations/deploy-llmd'))
for deployment, readiness checks, and rollback.
